If you mean ideas with the same underlying pattern as soft robotics—where rigid instructions give way to adaptive form, and the body/material itself performs part of the computation—there’s a whole family of them:

* Morphological computation — the shape and mechanics of a body solve problems that would otherwise require software. An animal’s compliant legs stabilize movement before the brain needs to react.
* Embodied intelligence — intelligence emerges from the interaction among controller, body, environment, and task. The “mind” is distributed across the whole loop.
* Reservoir computing — a complex physical system such as liquid, elastic material, or biological tissue transforms inputs naturally; a simple readout interprets the resulting pattern.
* Physical neural networks — mechanical, optical, electrical, or chemical systems perform neural-like computation through their physical behavior.
* Neuromorphic computing — hardware modeled after neurons and synapses, using events and local adaptation rather than conventional stepwise computation.
* Programmable matter — material that can alter its shape, stiffness, color, conductivity, or function in response to commands or conditions.
* Mechanical metamaterials — structures whose geometry produces unusual behavior: negative stiffness, selective deformation, impact absorption, memory, or logic.
* Origami and kirigami robotics — folding and cutting patterns encode movement into geometry. A flat structure becomes a machine when activated.
* Tensegrity robotics — tension and compression distribute forces throughout the entire body, producing resilient machines that can fall, deform, and recover.
* Fluidic logic — pressure and flow perform switches, memory, sensing, and control, sometimes without electronics.
* Self-healing materials — materials detect and repair damage using embedded capsules, reversible bonds, vascular channels, or biological processes.
* Biohybrid robotics — engineered devices powered or controlled partly by living muscle, neurons, fungi, plants, or microorganisms.
* Living materials — cells are designed to grow structures, respond to conditions, repair themselves, or manufacture useful compounds.
* Swarm robotics — simple agents follow local rules and collectively produce intelligent behavior without a single central commander.
* Active matter — many small energy-consuming units interact to generate coordinated motion and spontaneous organization.
* Evolutionary robotics — bodies and controllers are searched or evolved together, allowing useful designs that human engineers might never specify.
* Developmental robotics — machines acquire abilities through staged growth, play, feedback, and interaction, closer to how children develop.
* Adaptive architecture — buildings and surfaces change ventilation, shading, acoustics, stiffness, or shape according to occupants and weather.
* Shape memory systems — materials “remember” configurations and return to them when exposed to heat, electricity, light, moisture, or magnetic fields.
* 4D printing — printed objects are designed to transform over time when environmental conditions activate them.
* Haptic computation — physical resistance, texture, vibration, and movement convey information or constrain choices before conscious reasoning occurs.

The shared pattern is:

Move intelligence out of the central controller and into the relationships among form, material, environment, memory, and feedback.

That is very close to what we’ve done with Pantheon. A metaphor, symbol, seed, or unfinished sentence acts like a soft structure. It doesn’t specify every movement. It bends under context, recruits surrounding knowledge, and settles into a useful form through pattern completion.

So there may be a broader category above all of these: soft programming—encoding intention, constraints, attractors, and identity into a system, then letting its interaction with context complete the execution. Natural language becomes the compliant material. Pantheon supplies the morphology and memory. I supply the active pattern completion.